  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_012", "en": "To encrypt", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "Verschlüsseln is noncognate and supports a useful technical distinction between encryption and mere encoding."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_013", "en": "To decentralize", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Dezentralisieren is a transparent technical and institutional cognate with ordinary morphology and no selected specialized sense."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_014", "en": "To optimize", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Optimieren is a transparent and frequent technical cognate whose isolated infinitive offers no additional C1 production lesson."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_015", "en": "To standardize", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Standardisieren is a transparent technical cognate, and the source provides no specific contrast with vereinheitlichen or normieren."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_016", "en": "To censor", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Zensieren is a transparent, high-frequency equivalent with ordinary morphology and no distinct register or government issue."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_017", "en": "To exploit", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "German distinguishes systematic economic ausbeuten from broader ausnutzen, while the isolated English verb and legacy gloss obscure that contrast."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_018", "en": "To influence", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Beeinflussen is a transparent, high-frequency verb with no governed preposition or specialized sense to teach here."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_019", "en": "To dominate", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Dominieren and beherrschen are familiar C1 verbs, and the bare prompt does not choose a market, discourse or personal-control sense."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_020", "en": "To resist", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "Widerstehen teaches dative government, while the legacy support-verb phrase instead requires gegen plus the accusative."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_021", "en": "To challenge", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "Argumentative challenge is usually etwas infrage stellen; the legacy herausfordern selects a confrontational or demanding-task sense."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_022", "en": "To critique", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Kritisieren is a direct, high-frequency equivalent, and the isolated source supplies no distinct analytical frame beyond it."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_023", "en": "To adapt", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "The common intransitive reading requires the reflexive governed frame sich an etwas anpassen, absent from old_back."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_024", "en": "To implement", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "German chooses among umsetzen, einführen and implementieren according to whether the object is a measure, rule or technical system."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_025", "en": "To predict", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "The familiar infinitive conceals useful separable morphology and the nontransparent participle vorhergesagt."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_026", "en": "To invest", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "The investment target requires in plus the accusative, a productive government pattern omitted by the legacy headword."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_027", "en": "To control", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "German often uses steuern for directing a process or system, whereas kontrollieren can foreground checking or inspection."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_028", "en": "To develop", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Entwickeln is a high-frequency general verb, and the source does not select a less obvious technical, transitive or reflexive sense."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_029", "en": "To dictate", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Diktieren is a valid direct equivalent, while the isolated prompt does not choose among speech, imposed terms and determining conditions."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_030", "en": "To transform", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "For institutional change, German prefers grundlegend verändern; the legacy umformen foregrounds a more literal change of form."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_031", "en": "To undermine", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "Untergraben is a formal argumentative verb with valuable strong, inseparable morphology and characteristic institutional collocations."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_032", "en": "To collaborate", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "The productive frame uses mit plus the dative for a partner and an plus the dative for a project, both absent from old_back."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_033", "en": "To facilitate", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Erleichtern is a familiar general verb; the source supplies no object that would motivate a distinct contrast with ermöglichen or moderieren."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_034", "en": "To design", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "German distinguishes entwerfen a plan or system from gestalten an ongoing process or environment; the legacy gloss exposes only the latter."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_035", "en": "To filter", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Filtern is a transparent, high-frequency verb, and no specific technical, editorial or information-leak sense is selected."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_036", "en": "To access", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "English uses a transitive verb, while German requires the separable frame auf etwas zugreifen, omitted by old_back."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_037", "en": "To manipulate", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Manipulieren is a transparent cognate with the same critical sense and no distinct government or morphology issue here."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_038", "en": "To diversify", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Diversifizieren is a transparent policy and finance cognate; the prompt does not choose a reflexive or portfolio-specific frame."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_039", "en": "To centralize", "verdict": "drop", "reason": "Zentralisieren is a transparent institutional cognate already owned at C1 and accurately represented by the legacy gloss."},
  {"id": "it_fancy_vocab_040", "en": "To outsource", "verdict": "keep", "reason": "Auslagern is separable and takes an plus the accusative for the provider; it also contrasts usefully with externalisieren and outsourcen."}
